{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. The working tree contains only the intended changes: the one-line fix in `src/app.py`, the new regression test, and `ANSWER.md`.\n\n**Why the second publish returned 500:** `similarity()` (`src/app.py:30`) returns a Python `float`, and DynamoDB via boto3 refuses floats (`TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead` — exactly what's in `logs/error.log`). The first publish ever finds an empty table, so `similar` is `[]`, no float reaches `put_item`, and the write succeeds. Every publish after that computes real similarity scores, the float lands in the item, and the write fails. The bug was there from the first deploy — it just needed existing data to become visible. I reproduced this against the fake table before fixing: first publish OK, second publish fails with the same TypeError.\n\n**The fix (one line, `src/app.py:30`):**\n\n```python\nreturn Decimal(str(round(0.7 * tag_score + 0.3 * word_score, 4)))\n```\n\nThe `str()` matters — `Decimal(0.2333)` would store the binary-float artifact; `Decimal(str(...))` stores the exact rounded value. I used `Decimal` rather than the colleague's integer-percentage approach to preserve the existing 4-decimal precision (noted in `ANSWER.md` as a deliberate deviation). I audited the other item fields: `published_at` is already `int`, the rest are strings — the score was the only float producer.\n\n**So it can't come back:** added `test_second_publish_stores_similar_scores` to `tests/test_publish.py`, which seeds the table first — per the colleague's lesson that the empty-table path is exercised exactly once in production, so the populated path is the real one. Both tests pass, and a manual two-publish run now stores `{'slug': ..., 'score': Decimal('0.2333')}` on the second write.\n\n`ANSWER.md` documents the cause, the fix, and which parts of the colleague's note applied (diagnosis, computed-value audit, boundary conversion, seeded-record test — with the int-vs-Decimal deviation called out)."}
